## 3. REST API Design & Controller Standards

- **Return Types:** All controller methods MUST return a `ResponseEntity<T>` with explicit HTTP status codes (`HttpStatus.OK`, `HttpStatus.CREATED`, `HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT`, etc.).
- **Naming Conventions:**
  - Standard RESTful URIs using plural nouns (e.g., `/api/v1/alarms`, `/api/v1/alarms/{alarmId}`).
  - Lowercase endpoints separated by hyphens (kebab-case).
  - Explicit HTTP verbs (`@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@PatchMapping`, `@DeleteMapping`).
- **DTO Usage:** Controllers should accept and return Data Transfer Objects (DTOs), never raw database entities. Use Java 21 `record` types for immutable DTOs where applicable.
- **Validation:** Use Jakarta Validation annotations (`@Valid`, `@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`) on request bodies and query parameters.

```java
// âœ… CORRECT
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/alarms")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class AlarmController {

    private final AlarmService alarmService;

    @GetMapping("/{alarmId}")
    public ResponseEntity<AlarmResponseDto> getAlarmById(@PathVariable String alarmId) {
        AlarmResponseDto response = alarmService.findAlarmById(alarmId);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(response);
    }
}
```

